Grad Shafranov derivation
Emil HV
April 2026
1 Presentation
There are three main steps to the derivation, using
• B = ∇ × A (Basic vector potential)
• µ0J = ∇ × B (Maxwell Amp`ere - we study static equilibrium so the displacement current is irrelevant)
• J × B = ∇p (MHD)
The main assumption that we use is that of symmetry about the axis, whereby
∂
∂ϕ = 0
for all scalar fields considered. Note, however, that the cylindrical basis vectors ϕˆ and ˆr both retain their
dependency on ϕ, and we still have
∂ϕˆr = ϕˆ & ∂ϕϕˆ = −ˆr
This distinction is subtle, and important to keep track of as it leads to the appearance of the elliptic operator
∆∗ which is so central to this problem.
The second key remark is that we study only equilibrium positions, so we are in statics and ∂t ≡ 0.
