3 Discussion, notation, numerics
In many applications, a non-dimensionalization is put into place which absorbs the µ0, in which case the equation
writes a little cleaner as
∆˜ ∗ψ˜ = −r˜J˜
ϕ
with
J˜
ϕ = ˜r
dp˜
dψ˜
+
˜f
r˜
d
˜f
dψ˜
It’s clear now that this is a slightly modified Poisson equation with a nonlinear source term on the right
hand side. Many numerical methods can be used to solve this equation, in different contexts and with different
objectives. For example, one can implement a fixed boundary solver, in which the equilibrium shape of the
plasma is assumed. Then toy functions are picked for the pressure and f parameters, and ψ is computed in
each case. While this method is not always true to the physics due to the assumed physics, in a real tokamak
one can usually control to a good extent the pressure and toroidal field (f function), so it can be useful to
experiment with different shapes and coefficient weightings to gain insight on the effect of each parameter on
plasma behavior. Another technique, albeit particularly advanced and challenging to implement, consists in
holding a so-called ”free-boundary”. In this case a large box is taken around the plasma profile, and at each
iteration not only is the Grad-Shafranov equation linearized and solved, but also the new boundary of this result
is computed. This method takes into account the natural displacement of the plasma, leading to a very physical
description of its behavior. Further, in tokamaks such as JET, when tuning the device it can be useful to have
a way to compute the pressure profiles and toroidal field from experimental data. In this case, a special solver
is built to tackle this inverse problem, which uses polynomial interpolation to solve GS from the data, and then
deduces the p and f functions. In conclusion, even the static equilibrium conditions obtained from MHD lead
to rich numerical techniques used to gain real physical insight on plasma physics in tokamaks around the world.
4